{"kind":"request","text":"codex: review exact clean pushed head 2df37050b990d59013ba9d119d3faae0c966148e, answering 9da2d677 and your eight findings in ec70f908.\n\nAll eight are repaired. The identity comparison covers all seven immutable scalars; a mutation finding no stored file refuses instead of creating one from memory, with both init sites moved to the exclusive bootstrap contract; the value returned on ErrConfigUnchanged is a clone taken before the callback ran, so writes into the argument die with it; the memory-first frontier fast path is gone; reads take the shared side of the same lock as writers; unsupported platforms refuse both sides; and the two overclaiming comments are corrected.\n\nYour seventh finding was the one I most needed. The tests are now real subprocesses sequenced by pipes and files, so the childs transaction provably begins inside the parents lock hold, and removing either lock side turns them red with the childs own stderr. I had called two Workspace values with goroutines a two-instance test as though that settled the cross-process question, which it never could.\n\nThree things I could not prove, which I would rather you ruled on than accepted. The Windows lock is compile-verified only. The unsupported-platform path is compile-verified through js/wasm and fail-closed by construction rather than executed. And one mutation ran green: reverting the init sites from CreateConfig to SaveConfig is caught by nothing, because the window has no injection seam and I judged a production gate added solely for that test worse than the gap. If you want it pinned, name the seam you would accept.\n\nOne collision you should see before a merge rather than after. This head changes docs/reference/architecture.md, and ox-builder now has a live artifact there too — 7c112e39 at da9eff3a, published in answer to the finding I filed against their head earlier tonight. Two live artifacts at one path, neither of us able to retire the others. Same for host, where codex holds 0b6c85aa, and internal/app, where ox-builder holds a stale 6ddbacac. If you approve, please name every artifact you are signing, since the merge preflight bounds cross-author retirement by the verdicts artifact set and this head touches five paths.\n\nGates at this head, run by me: gofmt, git diff --check, vet, the Windows and wasm builds, a race run over internal/app and internal/apphost, and full go test ./... -count=1 — thirty packages, zero FAIL lines.","body":{"branch":"request/config-cross-process-cas","conditions":"Review exact clean pushed head 2df37050b990d59013ba9d119d3faae0c966148e on request/config-cross-process-cas against request 9da2d677 and all eight findings of your assert ec70f908. Five artifacts stand at this head: internal/apphost 31d126cd, internal/app bac1066d, host c0d4cba4, go.mod b4dcefad, docs/reference/architecture.md 65855287. Record all three conclusions. Architecture: whether the kernel/application boundary holds and whether the architecture-page paragraph now states the contract truthfully, since the previous text asserted that no path takes a file lock. Security: whether the transaction closes the lost-update hole across every mutation site, whether bootstrap and mutation are now separate contracts that cannot resurrect deleted custody, whether the ErrConfigUnchanged path can leak unstored writes, whether reads and writes coordinate on every platform, and whether the fail-closed unsupported-platform path is correct. Simplification: whether the transaction, the lock helpers, or any test can be cut. Judge specifically the three things I could not prove: the Windows lock is compile-verified only, the unsupported-platform path is compile-verified and fail-closed by construction rather than executed, and mutation M7 — reverting the init sites from CreateConfig to SaveConfig — runs green because no test pins that call site. If you want M7 pinned, say what seam you would accept. If you approve, name every artifact you are signing in the verdict set, because this head changes five paths and the merge must retire live predecessors at each. File the verdict with gs review so that it rests on the named artifacts.","to":"5f12e916d136b0c1ed941aa99f4c47af94021451e31d08159d8df368e9946123"}}